D={
"Winston Churchill":("Winston Churchill (1874–1965), a British aristocrat, soldier and journalist, served in India, Sudan and the Boer War before entering Parliament. As First Lord of the Admiralty he backed the disastrous Gallipoli campaign (1915). In the 1930s he warned against Hitler, and as Prime Minister from May 1940 he refused to negotiate with Nazi Germany, rallying Britain with speeches like 'We shall fight on the beaches'. He worked closely with Roosevelt and Stalin, won the 1953 Nobel Prize in Literature, and returned as PM in 1951–55. In India he is remembered for opposing independence, calling Gandhi a 'half-naked fakir', and for his war cabinet's role in the Bengal famine of 1943.","British Empire; World War I; World War II; Bengal famine of 1943; Franklin D. Roosevelt; Joseph Stalin; Adolf Hitler; Mahatma Gandhi; Christianity"),
"Franklin D. Roosevelt":("Franklin Delano Roosevelt (1882–1945), the only US president elected four times, took office in 1933 at the depth of the Great Depression. His New Deal created jobs programmes, Social Security, banking reforms and huge public works like the Tennessee Valley Authority, greatly expanding government's role. Paralysed from the waist down by polio at 39, he hid his disability from the public. He led the US into World War II after Pearl Harbor (1941), shaped the Atlantic Charter with Churchill, and planned the United Nations, but died in April 1945, weeks before victory in Europe. He also ordered the internment of Japanese Americans.","United States; Great Depression; World War II; United Nations founded; Winston Churchill; Joseph Stalin; Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki; Christianity"),
"Adolf Hitler":("Adolf Hitler (1889–1945), born in Austria, was a failed artist and World War I corporal who led the Nazi Party. Exploiting resentment over the Treaty of Versailles and the Great Depression, he became German Chancellor in 1933 and quickly made himself dictator. His regime persecuted Jews, Roma, disabled people and political opponents, rearmed Germany, and started World War II by invading Poland in 1939. Nazi Germany murdered six million Jews in the Holocaust. Facing defeat, Hitler killed himself in his Berlin bunker on 30 April 1945. He is history's most notorious example of how democracy can be destroyed from within.","World War II; The Holocaust; Great Depression; Battle of Stalingrad; Winston Churchill; Joseph Stalin; Franklin D. Roosevelt; Subhas Chandra Bose"),
"Joseph Stalin":("Joseph Stalin (1878–1953), born Ioseb Jughashvili in Georgia, was a Bolshevik revolutionary who rose to rule the Soviet Union from the late 1920s until his death. His five-year plans forced rapid industrialisation, and collectivisation of agriculture caused famines, including the Holodomor in Ukraine. The Great Purge (1936–38) executed about 750,000 people, and millions passed through the Gulag. He signed a pact with Hitler in 1939 but, after Germany invaded in 1941, led the USSR to victory at enormous cost, then imposed communist rule on Eastern Europe, beginning the Cold War.","Soviet Union; Russian Revolution; Holodomor; World War II; Battle of Stalingrad; Adolf Hitler; Winston Churchill; Franklin D. Roosevelt; Mao Zedong"),
"Mao Zedong":("Mao Zedong (1893–1976), son of a farmer in Hunan, co-founded the Chinese Communist Party and built a peasant-based revolution. He led the Long March (1934–35), fought Japan and won the civil war, proclaiming the People's Republic of China in 1949. Land reform and campaigns against 'enemies' followed. The Great Leap Forward (1958–62) caused a famine that killed perhaps 15–45 million people, and the Cultural Revolution (1966–76) unleashed Red Guards against intellectuals and officials. He unified China and raised literacy, but at a massive human cost.","Chinese Revolution; Chinese Civil War; Chinese Great Leap Forward famine; Qing dynasty; Joseph Stalin; Korean War; Sino-Indian War"),
"Martin Luther King Jr.":("Martin Luther King Jr. (1929–1968), a Baptist minister from Atlanta, led the US civil rights movement. Inspired by Gandhi's satyagraha (he visited India in 1959), he led the Montgomery bus boycott (1955–56) after Rosa Parks' arrest, the Birmingham campaign and the 1963 March on Washington, where he gave his 'I Have a Dream' speech. His work helped pass the Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965), and he won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964. He was assassinated in Memphis in 1968.","United States; Mahatma Gandhi; Christianity; American Civil War; Nelson Mandela"),
"Nelson Mandela":("Nelson Mandela (1918–2013), a Xhosa lawyer, co-founded the ANC Youth League and fought apartheid, South Africa's system of racial segregation. After the Sharpeville massacre (1960), he helped found the ANC's armed wing and was sentenced to life imprisonment in 1964, spending 27 years in jail, 18 of them on Robben Island. Released in 1990, he negotiated the end of apartheid with F. W. de Klerk, shared the Nobel Peace Prize (1993) and became South Africa's first Black president in 1994, promoting reconciliation. India awarded him the Bharat Ratna in 1990.","End of apartheid; Mahatma Gandhi; Martin Luther King Jr.; Decolonisation of Africa and Asia; Christianity"),
"Napoleon Bonaparte":("Napoleon Bonaparte (1769–1821), a Corsican artillery officer, rose through the French Revolution's wars, seized power in a coup in 1799 and crowned himself Emperor in 1804. His victories at Austerlitz, Jena and Wagram made him master of Europe; his Napoleonic Code established legal equality and property rights still influential worldwide. His invasion of Egypt (1798) aimed to threaten British India and led to the discovery of the Rosetta Stone. The invasion of Russia (1812) destroyed his army, and after defeat at Waterloo (1815) he died in exile on St Helena.","French Revolution; Napoleonic Wars; Battle of Waterloo; Bourbon dynasty (France); Holy Roman Empire; Haitian Revolution; Latin American independence; Tipu Sultan"),
"Queen Victoria":("Victoria (1819–1901) became Queen of the United Kingdom at 18 in 1837 and reigned for 63 years. After the 1857 rebellion, the Crown took over India, and in 1877 she became Empress of India; she learned Hindustani from her Indian attendant Abdul Karim. Her era saw industrial growth, railways, the expansion of the British Empire to a quarter of the world, but also famines in India and Ireland. Her nine children married into Europe's royal families, earning her the title 'grandmother of Europe'.","British Empire; British Raj; Indian Rebellion of 1857; Great Famine of 1876–78; Irish Great Famine; Christianity"),
"Rani Lakshmibai":("Lakshmibai (about 1828–1858), born Manikarnika in Varanasi, married the Raja of Jhansi. When he died in 1853, Governor-General Dalhousie used the Doctrine of Lapse to annex Jhansi, refusing to recognise their adopted son. In 1857 she joined the rebellion, defending Jhansi against Sir Hugh Rose's siege in 1858, then escaped with her son tied to her back and joined Tatya Tope to capture Gwalior. She died fighting near Gwalior's Phool Bagh in June 1858. Rose called her 'the most dangerous of all Indian leaders'.","Indian Rebellion of 1857; East India Company rule; Mangal Pandey; Maratha Confederacy; Hinduism"),
"Mangal Pandey":("Mangal Pandey (1827–1857), a sepoy of the 34th Bengal Native Infantry at Barrackpore near Calcutta, attacked British officers on 29 March 1857, angered by rumours that new Enfield rifle cartridges were greased with cow and pig fat, offending Hindus and Muslims. He was hanged on 8 April. His act preceded the Meerut uprising of 10 May, and he became a symbol of the 1857 rebellion; 'Pandy' became British slang for rebels.","Indian Rebellion of 1857; East India Company rule; Rani Lakshmibai; Hinduism"),
"Jawaharlal Nehru":("Jawaharlal Nehru (1889–1964), educated at Harrow and Cambridge, became Gandhi's close ally and a Congress leader, spending about nine years in prison. As India's first Prime Minister (1947–64), he built democratic institutions, a secular constitution, five-year plans, IITs, AIIMS and the atomic energy and space programmes. His 'Tryst with Destiny' speech marked independence. He led the Non-Aligned Movement, but the 1962 war with China damaged his reputation. He wrote The Discovery of India in jail.","Indian Independence and Partition; Indian Constitution adopted; Mahatma Gandhi; Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel; B. R. Ambedkar; Sino-Indian War; British Raj; Quit India Movement"),
"B. R. Ambedkar":("Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar (1891–1956), born into the Mahar caste, considered 'untouchable', overcame discrimination to earn doctorates from Columbia and the London School of Economics. He led the Mahad satyagraha (1927) for Dalits' right to use public water, and debated Gandhi over separate electorates (Poona Pact, 1932). As chairman of the Constituent Assembly's drafting committee, he shaped India's Constitution, abolishing untouchability. As law minister he pushed the Hindu Code Bill for women's rights. In 1956 he converted to Buddhism with about 500,000 followers, founding Navayana.","Indian Constitution adopted; Neo-Buddhism (Navayana); Buddhism; Mahatma Gandhi; Jawaharlal Nehru; British Raj"),
"Subhas Chandra Bose":("Subhas Chandra Bose (1897–1945), called Netaji, resigned from the Indian Civil Service to join the freedom movement and was elected Congress president in 1938 and 1939, but clashed with Gandhi over non-violence. Escaping house arrest in 1941, he sought help from Germany and then Japan, leading the Azad Hind government and Indian National Army in Southeast Asia with the slogan 'Give me blood, and I will give you freedom'. The INA fought at Imphal and Kohima. He reportedly died in a plane crash in Taiwan in 1945; the INA trials of 1945–46 inspired mass protests.","Battles of Imphal and Kohima; World War II; Quit India Movement; Mahatma Gandhi; Jawaharlal Nehru; Adolf Hitler; British Raj"),
"Bhagat Singh":("Bhagat Singh (1907–1931), from a Punjabi family of revolutionaries, was shaken by the Jallianwala Bagh massacre at 12. He joined the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association; to avenge Lala Lajpat Rai's death after a police beating, he and Rajguru shot police officer John Saunders in 1928. He and Batukeshwar Dutt threw non-lethal bombs into the Central Legislative Assembly in 1929 to 'make the deaf hear'. He wrote 'Why I Am an Atheist' in jail and was hanged with Rajguru and Sukhdev on 23 March 1931, aged 23.","Jallianwala Bagh massacre; British Raj; Mahatma Gandhi; Sikhism"),
"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel":("Vallabhbhai Patel (1875–1950), a lawyer from Gujarat, led the Kheda (1918) and Bardoli (1928) peasant satyagrahas, earning the title 'Sardar'. As independent India's first Deputy Prime Minister and Home Minister, he persuaded or pressured over 560 princely states to join India, using military action in Hyderabad (1948). He created the modern IAS. The 182-metre Statue of Unity in Gujarat honours him.","Indian Independence and Partition; Nizams of Hyderabad (Asaf Jahi); Mahatma Gandhi; Jawaharlal Nehru; Salt March; British Raj"),
"Sarojini Naidu":("Sarojini Naidu (1879–1949), the 'Nightingale of India', was a poet who published The Golden Threshold and joined the freedom struggle. She became the first Indian woman president of the Congress (1925), marched in the Salt March and was jailed during Quit India. After independence she became Governor of the United Provinces, India's first woman governor. National Women's Day is celebrated on her birthday.","Salt March; Quit India Movement; Mahatma Gandhi; British Raj"),
"Birsa Munda":("Birsa Munda (1875–1900), from the Munda tribe of Chota Nagpur, led the Ulgulan ('great tumult') of 1899–1900 against British rule, landlords, moneylenders and missionaries who took tribal land. He founded a religious movement, the Birsait faith, and was called 'Dharti Aba' (father of the earth). He died in Ranchi jail aged 25. His uprising led to the Chotanagpur Tenancy Act (1908); 15 November, his birthday, is Janjatiya Gaurav Divas and Jharkhand's foundation day.","British Raj; Santhal rebellion; Munda; Animism and shamanism"),
"Rajaraja Chola I":("Rajaraja I (r. 985–1014) transformed the Chola kingdom into an empire, conquering the Cheras and Pandyas, Vengi, Gangavadi, northern Sri Lanka (destroying Anuradhapura) and the Maldives with a powerful navy. He organised a detailed land survey and revenue system, and built the Brihadeeswarar temple at Thanjavur (1010), whose vimana rises about 66 m and whose walls record gifts and temple staff. His son Rajendra I extended his conquests to the Ganga and Southeast Asia.","Chola Empire; Chola dynasty (Imperial); Chola naval expedition to Srivijaya; Rajendra Chola's Ganga expedition; Hinduism; Srivijaya"),
"Razia Sultan":("Razia (r. 1236–1240), daughter of Iltutmish, was named heir by her father ahead of her brothers. The only woman to rule the Delhi Sultanate, she discarded the veil, wore a tunic and turban, held open court and led armies. Her promotion of the Abyssinian noble Jamal-ud-Din Yaqut angered the Turkish nobles (the 'Forty'). Governor Altunia of Bathinda rebelled and captured her; she married him and tried to regain Delhi, but both were killed in 1240.","Delhi Sultanate; Mamluk (Slave) dynasty; Islam"),
}
